WEBVTT

c0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen. I'd like to welcome each of you and all of you to the fifth presentation in the Solomon Goldman Lecture Series for academic year 1987-1988.

c0002
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The Solomon Goldman Lecture Series has been endowed in perpetuity in memory of the late Rabbi Solomon Goldman of Antiamet Synagogue here in Chicago.

c0003
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
For those of you who don't know me, I think most of you do, I'm Byron Sherwin, Vice President of Spertus College.

c0004
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
As I believe all of you know, even before the founding of the State of Israel, there was both...

c0005
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.980
Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen.

c0006
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:12.380
I'd like to welcome each of you and all of you to the fifth presentation in the Solomon Goldman Lecture Series for Academic Year 1987-1988.

c0007
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:22.460
The Solomon Goldman Lecture Series has been endowed in perpetuity in memory of the late Rabbi Solomon Goldman of Antiamet Synagogue here in Chicago.

c0008
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.220
For those of you who don't know me, I think most of you do, I'm Byron Sherwin, Vice President of Spurtus College.

c0009
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:41.120
As I believe all of you know, even before the founding of the State of Israel, there was both an Ashkenaz chief Rabbi and a Sepharadi chief Rabbi.

c0010
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:52.420
And as you may also know, Ashkenaz rabbis are called Rav, Master in Hebrew, and Sepharadi rabbis are called Hakham, Wise Man in Hebrew.

c0011
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Before the founding of the State of Israel, there was a chief Ashkenaz Rabbi, Rav Herzog, and a chief Sepharadi Rabbi, Hakham Uziel.

c0012
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:13.340
And the story is told that once they had a meeting at the Ministry of Religion, and after the meeting was over, each left in his respective limousine.

c0013
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.900
and on the road where the limousines passed,

c0014
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.760
there was a young Israeli girl, not what you'd call overdressed,

c0015
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.440
who was doing what many people do in Israel.

c0016
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.580
She was hitching a ride.

c0017
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.240
And the first limousine to come by was the limousine of the Ashkenaz chief Rabbi,

c0018
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.240
Rav Herzog.

c0019
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.300
The chauffeur looked out the window, saw the girl hitching,

c0020
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.520
saw how she was dressed, knew she needed a ride,

c0021
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.640
and turned around to Rav Herzog and said, should we give her a ride?

c0022
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.160
Rav Herzog, who was in the back studying at the time,

c0023
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.620
looked out the window, saw the girl,

c0024
00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.020
immediately pulled down the shades of the limousine and told the chauffeur to go ahead.

c0025
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.220
Ten minutes later, Hakham Uziel's limousine came by,

c0026
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.540
and before the chauffeur even had a chance to ask him whether to stop,

c0027
00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.080
He said to the chauffeur, stop the car.

c0028
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.740
He jumped out of the car, ushered the young lady into the car,

c0029
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.280
and she immediately, when she saw who it was, became very apprehensive and wanted to sit in the jumper seat.

c0030
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:24.100
He said, no, no, sit here next to me.

c0031
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.300
And then she was trying to figure out what she would talk to such a person about,

c0032
00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.440
and he began to talk to her about the fashions showing that season in Paris.

c0033
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.820
when they arrived at her destination

c0034
00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.680
Hakham Uziel got out of the car

c0035
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.900
opened the door for her

c0036
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.940
and before she left she said

c0037
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.100
you know I have to ask you

c0038
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.580
to explain something to me

c0039
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.800
why did Rav Herzog act the way he did

c0040
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.360
and you

c0041
00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.160
the chief Sepharadi Rabbi

c0042
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.080
Hakham Uziel act the way

c0043
00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:56.840
you did

c0044
00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.400
and he said to her

c0045
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.240
my child this is to teach you a very important lesson

c0046
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.020
and the important lesson

c0047
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.100
is the difference between a Rav and a Hakham.

c0048
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.940
A more elegant distinction between a Rav and a Hakham

c0049
00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:24.540
is found in the legal responsa of the 19th century scholar Rabbi Joseph Chaim of Baghdad.

c0050
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.160
The Talmud reports a case in which a certain scholar was called Hakham,

c0051
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.480
but was not also called Rabbi.

c0052
00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.980
and Rabbi Joseph Chaim of Baghdad explains

c0053
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.140
that Rabbi Rav denotes a person of erudition

c0054
00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:45.820
who is a master of applying logical analysis to the sources under his command.

c0055
00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.580
But a Hakham is one who goes even further than that

c0056
00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.420
in that a Hakham has not only mastered the sources

c0057
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.780
but can also treat them with independent judgment

c0058
00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.580
and with personal creativity

c0059
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:04.180
to arrive at innovative and precedent-making decisions in matters of law and theology.

c0060
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:12.340
It is my great pleasure and honor today to introduce to you a scholar who exemplifies the erudition,

c0061
00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:19.120
the analytic skills, and the innovative creativity both of a Rav and of a Hakham,

c0062
00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.520
namely our speaker today, Dr. Jose Faor.

c0063
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:34.440
I am especially pleased because today's lecture inaugurates his serving as a visiting professor at Spertus College of Judaica during the spring academic quarter.

c0064
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:41.840
In the classes he will offer and in the public lectures he will deliver, about which I'll have more to say after his presentation,

c0065
00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:50.060
you will have the opportunity to meet a scholar of international renown, a scholar of breathtaking breadth and depth of erudition,

c0066
00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:55.560
a scholar who has made remarkable creative contributions to Jewish learning,

c0067
00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:02.700
a scholar who combines depth and breadth of scholarship with inspired and stimulating pedagogy.

c0068
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.660
A native of Argentina, Professor Faor received his ordination as a Rabbi,

c0069
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.100
or as Barbara Walters would say, as a Jewish Rabbi,

c0070
00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:21.200
and subsequently as a Dayan, a rabbinic judge from the Sepharadi Beit Din of Jerusalem.

c0071
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.040
He is the first Jewish scholar to emerge from Spain since the Inquisition.

c0072
00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.380
He received his B.A., M.A., and Ph.D. degrees from the University of Barcelona,

c0073
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.840
or as he would probably say, Barthelona.

c0074
00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.760
He was the first Rabbi to teach at a Catholic seminary in Spain,

c0075
00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.820
and that was the Arch-Conservative Pontifical Seminary of Salamanca,

c0076
00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.120
the first Rabbi to teach there since the 15th century.

c0077
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:55.000
When Pope John XXIII ordered the removal of the term perfidious Jew from the Catholic liturgy,

c0078
00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.700
the Pope's secretary called on Professor Faora's erudition

c0079
00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:06.200
to help write the official Vatican document defending the Pope's action from the attacks of church conservatives.

c0080
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.120
and Dr. Faura's views are quoted extensively in this official Vatican document.

c0081
00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.060
Dr. Faura is the author of four magisterial books

c0082
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.760
and of more than 60 scholarly articles and monographs

c0083
00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.300
published around the world in four languages.

c0084
00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.760
A master himself of over ten languages and dialects,

c0085
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:31.380
Professor Faura is thoroughly conversant with such diverse fields as theology, philosophy, law,

c0086
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.440
comparative linguistics, political science, semiotics, and much more.

c0087
00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.780
Reading Dr. Faor's work, one travels from the intricacies of Talmudic argument

c0088
00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.720
to the theaters of 17th century Amsterdam,

c0089
00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.740
from the details of Aramaic grammar

c0090
00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.860
to the problems of medieval Jewish, Christian, and Muslim philosophy,

c0091
00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.640
from Maimonides' 12th century code to the contemporary writings of Derrida,

c0092
00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:02.940
from the Hebrew poets of the Middle Ages in Spain to Spinoza and the Conversos,

c0093
00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.120
from biblical theology to the Sepharadi roots of modern Zionism.

c0094
00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:14.260
In 17th century Poland, when a king died, the nobility would meet to elect a new king.

c0095
00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.700
One day it happened that the king died.

c0096
00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:22.580
And each week the nobles met, and one of the nobles spoke about his candidate to be king.

c0097
00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.580
An entire year passed, and a king had not yet been elected.

c0098
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.780
One day one of the nobles brought his candidate before the assembled nobles,

c0099
00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:38.360
and upon meeting him, rather than simply hearing about him, the nobles elected him king.

c0100
00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.260
The point of this story is that descriptions are always inadequate.

c0101
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.440
One has to meet the individual discussed in person,

c0102
00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:53.900
and so it is my great privilege and honor to present to you our visiting professor this semester, Dr. Jose Faor.

c0103
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.980
Professor Faor will speak today on the relationship of Jewish law and Jewish ethics.

c0104
00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:02.840
Please welcome him to Chicago and to the college.

c0105
00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:11.920
Thank you very much, Dr. Sherwin.

c0106
00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:18.140
Ladies, gentlemen, Jewish law and Jewish ethics.

c0107
00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.380
In the modern world, specifically in the Protestant tradition,

c0108
00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.800
there are two different classes of duties.

c0109
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:40.920
Law stands for those obligations imposed upon us by an outside authority, by something from

c0110
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:49.080
without. Ethics comprises those duties that come from the inner self of the individual.

c0111
00:09:49.080 --> 00:10:06.680
Law implies authority. Ethics imply freedom. Accordingly, the legal duties stand for something

c0112
00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:15.280
peripheral to the individual, something that really does not pertain to the very self.

c0113
00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:25.580
Thus ethics, morality, express the very essence of what an individual is.

c0114
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:37.100
Therefore, there is an opposition between the outer, the legal, the inner, the ethical,

c0115
00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:43.700
what Protestant theologians, Luther, Calvin,

c0116
00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:48.560
refer to as the unfreedom of the law

c0117
00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.600
versus the freedom of ethics.

c0118
00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.160
Judaism does not recognize such a distinction

c0119
00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:03.780
between law and ethics.

c0120
00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:17.880
for Judaism law and ethics are not two exclusive thoughts rather they complement one another if

c0121
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:28.200
you want like the two sides of a coin in order to understand what Judaism means by both law

c0122
00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:37.200
Law and ethics. Why is it that Judaism considers these two ideas as complementary to one another?

c0123
00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:44.600
We must understand the concept of authority in non-Jewish tradition.

c0124
00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:52.560
In the West, authority is intimately connected with the idea of hierarchy.

c0125
00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.640
The universe, the physical universe.

c0126
00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:09.620
as well as society, political institutions, are viewed as a scale in which there are superiors and inferiors.

c0127
00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:23.480
Duty, obligation, is the fiat by which a superior exercises his authority over the inferior.

c0128
00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:37.280
since everyone is either an inferior or superior everyone owes authority to someone authority is

c0129
00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:46.520
imposed precisely because it comes from the superior towards the inferior now this idea

c0130
00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:51.680
became part and parcel of Christianity,

c0131
00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:58.060
more particularly of the organization of the Christian church.

c0132
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.760
Borrowing a Roman legal principle,

c0133
00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.940
Christianity conceived Christian individuals

c0134
00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:09.880
as members of what is called in Christian theology

c0135
00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:14.480
Corpus Christi, the body of Christ.

c0136
00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:25.620
through baptism, an individual loses his own individuality, becomes part of a cosmic body,

c0137
00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:35.720
in which body the different parts represent the hierarchy, the superiority of one over the other.

c0138
00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:47.280
Beyond the greatest expert on Christian canon law, Professor Walter Oldman from Cambridge

c0139
00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:55.560
University in England, he writes as follows, by virtue of his baptism and consequential

c0140
00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:02.920
incorporation into the church and corpus Christi, the individual Christian loses his autonomous

c0141
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:13.240
character and becomes a fidelis subditus, means a faithful subject. Now, how does a

c0142
00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:26.160
subject expresses his faith? A subject expresses his faith simply by obeying his superior.

c0143
00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:36.140
to a superior becomes the most important aspect of Christian faith. Again, I am quoting Professor

c0144
00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:44.960
Ullman. The consequence of the incorporation into the Corpus Christi was that his fedilitas,

c0145
00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:53.740
his faithfulness, consisted precisely in the obeying the law of those who were instituted

c0146
00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:56.360
over him by divinity.

c0147
00:14:57.740 --> 00:14:59.600
The individual became absorbed

c0148
00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.680
by the corporation itself,

c0149
00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:03.020
by the church,

c0150
00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.080
which itself, however, was governed

c0151
00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:08.000
under monarchic principle

c0152
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.320
according to which authority

c0153
00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.120
comes from the head.

c0154
00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.160
There is one head,

c0155
00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.260
and from this head,

c0156
00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:18.660
all authority filters down.

c0157
00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:21.760
Pope Gregory the Great,

c0158
00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:25.080
perhaps the most important

c0159
00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.940
legal

c0160
00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:27.620
thinker

c0161
00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:30.600
in canon

c0162
00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.160
history

c0163
00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.460
in the 6th century

c0164
00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:36.580
he stated the following

c0165
00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.940
that the verdict of the

c0166
00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:39.400
superior

c0167
00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:42.760
no matter whether it was just or

c0168
00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:43.380
unjust

c0169
00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:47.080
had to be obeyed by the inferior subject

c0170
00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.060
and he said

c0171
00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:50.720
that faith

c0172
00:15:50.720 --> 00:16:00.660
again consists in the absolute obedience of the inferior to the superior.

c0173
00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:06.900
Now, the whole notion of a hierarchical Corpus Christi,

c0174
00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:13.820
in which the inferior must accept the authority of the superior,

c0175
00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:16.320
appears already in Paul.

c0176
00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:20.980
In the Ephesians 5, 22, 24,

c0177
00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:25.180
in which addressing the Christian wives,

c0178
00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.020
he says to them,

c0179
00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.820
subject yourself to your own husbands,

c0180
00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.260
as to the Lord, as to God.

c0181
00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:36.520
Because a husband is the head of the wife,

c0182
00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:39.940
as also Christ is head of the church,

c0183
00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.220
and he is the saver of the body.

c0184
00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:56.460
hierarchy is the ground for authority. Law represents again the duty of accepting the

c0185
00:16:56.460 --> 00:17:07.320
opposition as an inferior. Now one of the interesting legal consequences of this theory

c0186
00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:17.380
is what Pope Gregory the Great designates as the inequality before the law.

c0187
00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:26.840
Individuals are unequal before the law because each one has rights and duties

c0188
00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:32.200
according to his or her position within that scale of value.

c0189
00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:45.500
Again, because there is inequality before the law, an inferior may not use the law against

c0190
00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:46.500
a superior.

c0191
00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:57.960
He has no right before the court to challenge on legal grounds anybody else's decision.

c0192
00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:12.360
Now, Pope Gregory justified that theory on the basis that although in nature things appear to be equal, in the realm of mankind we have merits.

c0193
00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:29.720
Merits means that we are born with certain grace that put us above somebody or again with less grace and therefore we are below somebody.

c0194
00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:38.720
This point, incidentally, if you allow me a digression, was challenged by a very great Jewish thinker.

c0195
00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:43.720
He was born in Italy in the 1820s. He died in 1900.

c0196
00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:49.720
Elie Ben Amosek, which has an extraordinary book called Moral Juive, Moral Chrétienne,

c0197
00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:55.720
in which compares Jewish ethics with moral ethics, with the Christian ethics,

c0198
00:18:55.720 --> 00:19:00.380
And the whole notion of imputing merits or guilt,

c0199
00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.260
by imputing means merits and guilt which are not connected with actions

c0200
00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.800
and which stem from the whole theory of Gregory the Great,

c0201
00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:12.320
he considers this as the most critical point

c0202
00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:17.660
as to the difference between Jewish ethics and Christian ethics.

c0203
00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:24.780
But again, the whole notion of inequality becomes part of Christian tradition

c0204
00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:29.580
and therefore again, law is always associated with something negative.

c0205
00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:37.220
The essential point of Judaism, the basis of everything for the Jew,

c0206
00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:40.960
is the concept of a Berit, of a covenant.

c0207
00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:49.380
According to scripture, God and Israel contracted a bilateral agreement

c0208
00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.080
at the foot of Mount Sinai,

c0209
00:19:52.080 --> 00:20:02.780
whereby the subsequent misva, 613 commandments, become the norm of the Jewish people.

c0210
00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:06.520
There is something very, very important about this covenant.

c0211
00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.540
Number one, which is the most important to us,

c0212
00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:19.040
the covenant sees mankind as something that is absolutely horizontal,

c0213
00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:29.700
Whereas in the hidden world, and later on in Christianity, mankind is seen vertically

c0214
00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:37.940
as an expression of a hierarchy of some superior to inferior, within the concept of Berit,

c0215
00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:44.640
everyone stands at a horizontal position.

c0216
00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:53.800
are no superiors or no inferiors. Therefore, even God himself, according to Jewish law,

c0217
00:20:53.800 --> 00:21:03.880
is bound by Deberit. God is not above Deberit, but is part of Deberit, and therefore God

c0218
00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:12.960
is limited by Deberit. One of the most amazing consequences of this theory was pointed already

c0219
00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:21.060
by Moshe Zilberg, the famous Supreme Court judge of Israel, who pointed out that in Judaism,

c0220
00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:26.940
God is a legal person. There is an interesting passage, and I'll show you how, in the Palestinian

c0221
00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:37.460
Talmud, in which God is contrasted with a hidden monarch. Using a Greek saying,

c0222
00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:44.780
Para basileos, nomos agrafos, means for the king, the law is non-existent.

c0223
00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:53.660
The Talmud compares a monarch who is not bound by the laws that he promotes with God,

c0224
00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:59.180
that according to Jewish tradition, is bound by the same laws of the pact.

c0225
00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:03.820
Because God is bound by the laws of the pact.

c0226
00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:13.820
In Jewish tradition, nobody could invoke divine revelation in his consultation of the law.

c0227
00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:24.820
There is an extraordinary passage in the Talmud, Baba Mesi'a, page 59, second century, late second century of the coming era,

c0228
00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.820
in which the rabbis are discussing a matter of law.

c0229
00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:34.920
law. The majority says something about the law, A, and the minority system, the individual

c0230
00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:41.940
says B. Now this individual did not want to accept the position of the majority, and he

c0231
00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:48.880
invokes God. And thereupon, a miracle happens, several kinds of miracles. The rabbis refused

c0232
00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:57.940
to accept the miracles. Finally, a divine voice is heard in which says, the halakha,

c0233
00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:06.060
the law is like Rabbi Eliezer, like this minority. And thereupon, the representative of the majority

c0234
00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:15.340
stands up and says, the law is not in heaven. God cannot intervene in the discussions of

c0235
00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:26.760
the judges. Now, not only that the position of God was rejected, but the judge in question,

c0236
00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:36.900
the minority, was banned because he tried to coerce the judges by using divine intervention.

c0237
00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:41.780
Now, this is an epoch-making decision.

c0238
00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:51.860
One of the implications of this theory is that if somebody would claim that God had told them something concerning any matter of law,

c0239
00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:56.020
my mother says this person should be charged as a false prophet,

c0240
00:23:56.340 --> 00:24:05.100
because Judaism cannot accept that God would disrespect the standing of the court.

c0241
00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:06.280
All right?

c0242
00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:18.120
Now, not only God is a legal person and therefore cannot alter a legal decision or change legal procedure,

c0243
00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:27.420
but what is more important, that judges, as members of the court, are also legal persons.

c0244
00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:29.100
Let me explain.

c0245
00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:33.420
And I think that there is no precedent to this in any other legal system.

c0246
00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:48.120
If a judge renders a decision in the view of the person who received the verdict, this judge made a capital error.

c0247
00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:54.340
it's called To'ebi Dvar Mishna, in other words, he overlooked a non-published source,

c0248
00:24:55.280 --> 00:25:03.740
the person has the right to sue the individual judge for compensation.

c0249
00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:07.200
In other words, you do not even have to overthrow the case.

c0250
00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:19.720
you sue the judge, yes, is liable for the damage that he caused to the individual with his or her decision.

c0251
00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.440
But there's something more than that.

c0252
00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:34.420
In our American legal tradition, by definition, the Supreme Court cannot make an error, cannot make a legal error.

c0253
00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:40.680
law is that which the Supreme Court declares said to be.

c0254
00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:47.760
In the scripture, we have two passages speaking about a court,

c0255
00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:53.160
specifically a Supreme Court, making an error.

c0256
00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:57.440
There's a whole tractate, Talmud Horayot,

c0257
00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:04.000
dealing with the procedure by which a court, a Supreme Court,

c0258
00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:13.060
Yes? Must admit and recognize the error. Of course, it involves a sacrifice.

c0259
00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:20.140
The, according to Jewish tradition, the Supreme Court, all the members of the Supreme Court,

c0260
00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:27.260
once that they admit the error, they have to bring a sacrifice of expiation to the temple.

c0261
00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.540
The importance of it is twofold.

c0262
00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:37.620
Number one, the law is independent of the Supreme Court.

c0263
00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:53.080
And number two, when accepting an error, the court is duty-bound to make its error public by bringing a sacrifice to the temple.

c0264
00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:57.440
In other words, the importance is that the error has to be admitted publicly.

c0265
00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:11.860
Now, because Jews see the law as horizontal, everybody, including God, including the officers of the court, are bound and limited by the law.

c0266
00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:21.420
I prefer to describe Judaism not as a theocracy, but I have a different term, nomocracy.

c0267
00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:28.860
Nomos, the law, is the only source of authority.

c0268
00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:30.440
It is the law.

c0269
00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:31.440
It is not God.

c0270
00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:34.640
It is not a theocratic government.

c0271
00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:37.820
It is a nomocratic government.

c0272
00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:47.000
Now, since the Berith, the covenant, involves consent, therefore there is a major difference

c0273
00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:54.240
between what the Christian demands of the individual faithful and what the Jew demands.

c0274
00:27:55.540 --> 00:28:02.440
For Christianity, of course, the subject has to have faith in the institutions governing him or her.

c0275
00:28:03.540 --> 00:28:07.220
In Judaism, it's a matter of consent.

c0276
00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:15.300
Several times, the scripture says that upon giving the law, the Jewish people say,

c0277
00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:17.040
naaseh v'nishma.

c0278
00:28:17.920 --> 00:28:18.840
We consent.

c0279
00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:19.960
We consent.

c0280
00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:20.860
We consent.

c0281
00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:22.920
There's a beautiful

c0282
00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.520
legend

c0283
00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.500
in the Midrash

c0284
00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.180
and the Song of Songs

c0285
00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.220
describing what I call

c0286
00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:33.020
the horizontality

c0287
00:28:33.020 --> 00:28:35.200
of the Jewish law.

c0288
00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:38.440
When God spoke

c0289
00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:40.660
in Mount Sinai,

c0290
00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:41.740
the rabbis say,

c0291
00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:44.380
His speech

c0292
00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:51.940
bifurcated into 600,000 branches.

c0293
00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:54.720
And to each individual,

c0294
00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:58.320
which were the 600,000 Jews at the foot of Mount Sinai,

c0295
00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.740
the word of God would bifurcate to him

c0296
00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:04.780
and speak at his level

c0297
00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:07.360
and reason with him

c0298
00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:09.560
and showing the implications.

c0299
00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:11.620
And then it would ask him,

c0300
00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:13.160
do you accept me?

c0301
00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:17.080
And the individual would say, yes, I do.

c0302
00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:18.900
Horizontality.

c0303
00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:23.120
By the way, if you allow me a digression,

c0304
00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:30.940
horizontality stands on one of the most beautiful ceremonies that we Jewish people have, the seder.

c0305
00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:34.920
In the seder, when we drink the four glasses of wine,

c0306
00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:36.840
and when we eat the matzah,

c0307
00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:40.500
we must recline on our left side.

c0308
00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:42.260
Why?

c0309
00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:45.520
within the etiquette of the time

c0310
00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:46.260
this means

c0311
00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:50.280
there is nobody above us

c0312
00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:52.460
only somebody who is free

c0313
00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:55.220
from a superior authority

c0314
00:29:55.220 --> 00:30:00.140
could recline to his left side

c0315
00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:01.980
something very beautiful

c0316
00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:03.780
in the Ashkenaz tradition

c0317
00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:05.700
the Ashkenaz tradition

c0318
00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:07.800
therefore insists that women too

c0319
00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:11.440
should recline to the left

c0320
00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:17.220
because a woman should not be considered a subservient to the husband.

c0321
00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:25.660
Mind you, the first person to speak about it was the Rabia Rabi Eliezer and Yoela Levi, 12th century Germany.

c0322
00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.400
Could you imagine 12th century Germany?

c0323
00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:32.700
Somebody saying that a wife is not subservient to the husband?

c0324
00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:35.020
This is modernity.

c0325
00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:50.760
Now, going back to the idea of consent, because for the Jew, the faithful Jew expresses his faith by consent rather than submission,

c0326
00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:57.580
there is a special relationship between the covenant and the people.

c0327
00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:04.020
The covenant is always in constant interpretation.

c0328
00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:10.560
To fulfill a misva, a norm of the pact.

c0329
00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:13.800
In Hebrew you say the word lekayem.

c0330
00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:18.860
Lekayem actually means to establish, to constitute.

c0331
00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:23.880
If you're doing something, whatever you're doing, as a part of the covenant,

c0332
00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:29.540
at that very moment you are establishing the covenant.

c0333
00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:33.020
and therefore there is always

c0334
00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:36.440
the matter of interpretation

c0335
00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:40.180
in other words it's not only the given covenant

c0336
00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:43.060
but it's also you

c0337
00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:46.880
as the fulfiller of the covenant

c0338
00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:49.180
interpreting the covenant

c0339
00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:51.060
and therefore the law

c0340
00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:54.740
is not something static

c0341
00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:58.080
but it's always dynamic

c0342
00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:01.960
showing an element of interpretation.

c0343
00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:04.320
Let me give you some examples.

c0344
00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:12.920
First of all, there is a beautiful Ladino term

c0345
00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:16.900
to designate the oral tradition.

c0346
00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:21.240
We always say that, of course, the scripture has to be interpreted according to oral tradition.

c0347
00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:28.080
But how would you translate the word oral tradition in terms which are meaningful to us?

c0348
00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:30.480
In Ladino,

c0349
00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:33.900
You say,

c0350
00:32:35.540 --> 00:32:38.080
The mental law.

c0351
00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:40.840
You know what the oral tradition is?

c0352
00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:45.600
Are those canons of thinking that make you an individual.

c0353
00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:49.760
And therefore, whenever we are reading the law,

c0354
00:32:49.760 --> 00:33:02.760
We are in point of fact applying to that law the mental regular norms that makes you what you are.

c0355
00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:09.760
So there is always a participation. There is no such thing as a purely divine law and purely human law.

c0356
00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:13.760
The divine law, it becomes human because you are interpreting it.

c0357
00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:26.220
And part of the task of the Jewish court is to alleviate us from the anachronisms of any static system.

c0358
00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:28.560
Let me give you two concrete examples.

c0359
00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:36.020
You're familiar with the eye for an eye law of the Pentateuch.

c0360
00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:45.020
The Pentateuch says, if somebody damages an eye, that person must pay with an eye, an

c0361
00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:47.520
Ein Be'er Tahat Ein.

c0362
00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:59.240
Come the rabbis and they say, no, it means that you must pay rather than to remove the

c0363
00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:00.900
eye.

c0364
00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:08.860
we have a very important precedent in which the law is interpreted to reflect certain

c0365
00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:19.240
type of advancement that a primitive society had gone through. Here, the role of interpretation

c0366
00:34:19.240 --> 00:34:28.520
is actually of eliminating an anachronism of the law. Let me give you another example.

c0367
00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:28.860
All right?

c0368
00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:32.560
The other example concerns the rebellious son.

c0369
00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:36.840
According to Jewish law, according to biblical law,

c0370
00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:42.620
if a son is rebellious, the parents have the right to put this child to death.

c0371
00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:48.820
Now, in an advanced society, this is criminal.

c0372
00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:56.520
The law of the Talmud makes it so impossible.

c0373
00:34:57.600 --> 00:34:57.720
Yes?

c0374
00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:04.020
To execute this law, that in point of fact, the Chachmeat Talmud, the rabbis end up and says,

c0375
00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:09.340
It could never, never be applied.

c0376
00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:15.680
Again, through interpretation, the law is constantly changed.

c0377
00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:20.340
In other words, you have here the law as being man and God.

c0378
00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:22.480
Let me give you a better example.

c0379
00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:31.520
At the beginning of the coming era, the rabbis come out with an extraordinary new law pertaining criminal cases.

c0380
00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:42.420
Nobody could be punished in a criminal court unless this individual was forewarned in advance of the crime.

c0381
00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:48.300
Not only do you have to forewarn this individual in general terms, but you have to cite chapter and verse.

c0382
00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:52.620
You have to tell them exactly what you're making, what kind of transgression you're making.

c0383
00:35:52.740 --> 00:36:02.840
Of course, the purpose of it was simply because many rabbis thought that any kind of capital punishment or corporeal punishment was brutal.

c0384
00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:15.160
So again, here comes the test of the judge through interpretation to circumvent or to solve the anachronism and injustices of the law.

c0385
00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:26.840
Let me compare what we mentioned before with a case brought by Benjamin Cardoso, the famous Supreme Court judge, a Jew, referring to a simple case.

c0386
00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:44.120
According, of course, if you drive your car and there is over a railroad and you didn't pay attention, yes, and then something happened to the car or to you, you are contributing the negligence.

c0387
00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:50.220
And, of course, there's a special case to treat it because you are contributing to the negligence because you drove without paying attention.

c0388
00:36:51.080 --> 00:37:01.160
According to the law in New York, it was not only the person who drives was considered negligent, but also the person sitting next to the driver.

c0389
00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:12.620
Now, I'd also, of course, point out that this is very unfair because if you're going to tell, I mean, nobody wants the person next to him to tell him what to do or what not to do, how to drive, what not to drive.

c0390
00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:17.080
that would actually contribute to the accident.

c0391
00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:22.340
And yet, he found it terribly difficult to overthrow that particular law.

c0392
00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:29.160
So, in other words, here you have something that is perennially true in a system of law,

c0393
00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:36.440
that laws need and require a dynamic kind of interpretation.

c0394
00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:41.380
Now, one of the consequences of this is that, according to Maimonides,

c0395
00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:48.140
no law therefore is either totally human or totally divine the whole distinction

c0396
00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:53.440
between divine and human laws according to my eyes is inoperative why on the one

c0397
00:37:53.440 --> 00:38:00.680
hand you have even when you have the law of God you need the mental laws the

c0398
00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:07.120
mental attitude of the individual that makes it to be a law furthermore according

c0399
00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:14.040
to Judaism, even the laws of the state, the civil laws, are part of the covenant. So therefore

c0400
00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:19.000
they are, in a sense, divine. And now we come to one of the most interesting aspects of

c0401
00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:24.300
Judaism. Not only that there is no difference between laws into two categories, divine and

c0402
00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.620
human, every human law has something of the divine, every divine has something of the

c0403
00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:35.720
human, but within the same legal system, we have things that in Western tradition may

c0404
00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:41.060
be classified as totally human laws in

c0405
00:38:41.060 --> 00:38:42.860
consideration to the ethical ones. How?

c0406
00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:47.240
Very important. Part of the covenant for

c0407
00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:51.080
the Jew is not only to give charity, it's

c0408
00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:54.920
a misva, but also to pay your debt is a

c0409
00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:58.820
misva. Not only according to the Jewish law,

c0410
00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:02.420
part of the covenant is to pray to God,

c0411
00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:07.340
but also to return faithfully a deposit.

c0412
00:39:08.920 --> 00:39:18.980
And here you see that the whole notion of ethics versus legal is non-existent.

c0413
00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:21.420
Before I go into the second stage, let me summarize.

c0414
00:39:22.920 --> 00:39:27.180
In Western tradition, we have a vertical hierarchical system of values

c0415
00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:30.600
by which the superior imposes his will on the inferior.

c0416
00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:36.540
Because of this, there are certain duties which are considered legal, they come from the outside,

c0417
00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:41.760
in contradistinction to those duties that come out of your inner self, the ethical one.

c0418
00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:49.200
For Judaism, there are no such a thing as vertical duties. Any duty is part of a

c0419
00:39:49.200 --> 00:39:56.340
covenant. In a covenant, everybody is part of it, and it depends upon the consent of the

c0420
00:39:56.340 --> 00:40:01.880
individual. There is a kind of a partnership, a constant partnership.

c0421
00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:09.060
Furthermore, because Judaism does not consider men as a composite of different

c0422
00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:13.900
things but in its totality, Judaism considers therefore that the salvation

c0423
00:40:13.900 --> 00:40:19.660
of men and society does not depend only on the spiritual in contrast to the

c0424
00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:23.880
material, or the material in contrast to the spiritual, but all those aspects of

c0425
00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:30.120
of mankind must be guarded, etc., by the law.

c0426
00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:34.340
Now, let us take a second point.

c0427
00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:36.120
Although Judaism formally does not

c0428
00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:40.400
recognize any difference between the legal and the ethical,

c0429
00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:44.300
still there are some things that in our language today we

c0430
00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:47.000
can consider as purely ethical.

c0431
00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:49.520
Before I analyze this ethical aspect of Judaism

c0432
00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:52.480
and how this ethical aspect is possible,

c0433
00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.840
Let me distinguish between two classes of ethics.

c0434
00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:59.500
One type of ethics I call devotional ethics.

c0435
00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:03.080
It's what in modern Hebrew we call musar.

c0436
00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:08.420
It's the ethics in which an individual regulates his or her life,

c0437
00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:13.320
his approaches to God, his approach to creation.

c0438
00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:17.740
And this word, which in Hebrew is musar,

c0439
00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:22.080
very interesting, in Ladino, we translate it as cultura.

c0440
00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:24.120
which means culture.

c0441
00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:28.320
And I checked, of course, in Arabic, in Judeo-Arabic,

c0442
00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:32.000
you translate it as adab, which is another word for culture.

c0443
00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:35.680
And in the Septuagint, the Septuagint in the Greek translation,

c0444
00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:38.080
we have the word paideos.

c0445
00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:41.760
In other words, paideia is the culture.

c0446
00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:44.740
This kind of musar, this kind of ethics,

c0447
00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:48.660
that you consider as part of his own individual social culture.

c0448
00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:56.400
In addition to this, there is a different kind of ethics, what I call judicial ethics.

c0449
00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:03.600
Now, how judicial ethics could be possible? And this is very important. It could be possible

c0450
00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:13.500
when the limits, with our case at hand, transcends the limits of the law. How could it be possible?

c0451
00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:20.380
First, it's possible because in the Jewish mind and the Jewish legal system, only those

c0452
00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:28.180
who accepted the Pact, in other words, those who are of the Jewish faith, are duty-bound

c0453
00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:34.500
by the Pact, and therefore they are protected by the Pact.

c0454
00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:38.880
Outside this Pact, nobody has the obligation to do anything.

c0455
00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:44.920
And because he has no obligations, the law cannot possibly guarantee or protect this

c0456
00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:45.920
individual.

c0457
00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:50.240
Now, let me give you a simple example.

c0458
00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.240
Charity.

c0459
00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:59.740
Charity, not only a person has the right to accept charity, the right to take charity,

c0460
00:42:59.740 --> 00:43:04.980
but also he has the obligation, when that individual has, to give charity.

c0461
00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:08.680
So therefore the privilege or the merits of charity, it's a two-way street.

c0462
00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:11.500
Now from the point of view of jurisprudence,

c0463
00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:15.180
you cannot give charity or you're not obligated to give charity

c0464
00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:18.460
to somebody who is not part of the legal system.

c0465
00:43:19.720 --> 00:43:22.420
Now what to do then for with a Gentile?

c0466
00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:26.840
Shall we say that you are obligated to give charity to a Gentile?

c0467
00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:31.300
Then you are in a very precarious situation because then next time you would say to the Gentile,

c0468
00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:33.120
you must also give us charity.

c0469
00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:38.020
Shall we say that you don't have to give charity to the Gentiles?

c0470
00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:39.940
Well, this is very inhuman.

c0471
00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:41.440
What to do?

c0472
00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:46.460
The Rambis promulgated a whole system of legislation called

c0473
00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:50.980
something which is Hayabim Mishpeneh Darkeh Shalom.

c0474
00:43:52.100 --> 00:43:54.600
It's a matter of living in peace with humanity,

c0475
00:43:55.060 --> 00:43:56.400
harmony with humanity.

c0476
00:43:56.400 --> 00:44:00.720
And on the basis of a purely ethical notion,

c0477
00:44:01.540 --> 00:44:03.940
the notion of Shalom Imaberiot,

c0478
00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:16.980
Rabbis, legislators, promulgate laws which in point of fact duty bound a Jew to extend to a Gentile the same kind of humanitarian...

c0479
00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:23.980
A hidden holiday. He's doing something which is contrary to the Jewish law.

c0480
00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:31.980
You cannot help this individual because, strictly speaking, this individual is outside the perimeter of the law

c0481
00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:35.300
And then in a sense you are helping an adversary.

c0482
00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:39.480
Now, this is from the point of pure theory.

c0483
00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:45.600
But then the legislators come up with an extraordinary idea.

c0484
00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:49.120
It says, certain actions should be forbidden.

c0485
00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:51.440
Mishum eva.

c0486
00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:55.820
Anything that promotes hatred between individuals.

c0487
00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:00.240
And hatred here is a purely legal notion.

c0488
00:45:01.980 --> 00:45:03.480
It is to be forbidden.

c0489
00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:06.140
So here you have something very important.

c0490
00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:17.260
There are individuals outside the scope of the Berit that could be sometimes harmed or be excluded from benefits.

c0491
00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:22.520
So what is then your legal basis to protect these individuals

c0492
00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:32.820
and to have them also partake in your beneficiary institutions.

c0493
00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:40.200
Morality becomes the basis of legislation to protect the Gentile.

c0494
00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:43.080
And to have the Gentile accept the same thing.

c0495
00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:45.420
A set note, if I may tell you.

c0496
00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:55.660
A 16th century Rabbi from Saloniki, Joseph Molho, Dr. Sherwin took me to the library

c0497
00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:58.760
and I saw that we have his books here, Shulchan Gavoha.

c0498
00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:04.980
He says the following, according to Jewish law, of course, we are duty bound to render

c0499
00:46:04.980 --> 00:46:09.460
assistance to the sick of the Gentile world.

c0500
00:46:09.460 --> 00:46:14.860
Now, the Jews had great doctors, came from Spain, and they had very excellent, for the

c0501
00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:26.480
time, hospital systems. And he says, we tried several times to approach the Greek population

c0502
00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:37.060
and says, the hatred is so great that we must refrain from offering them any kind of medical

c0503
00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:43.460
assistance because otherwise they will hate us more. He says, mishum eva, we have to prevent

c0504
00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:47.380
ourselves to give charity because the more charity you give to them or you try to give

c0505
00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:53.560
to them, the more you are hated by them. It's a very sad note. Incidentally, if you permit

c0506
00:46:53.560 --> 00:47:01.780
me to with this sad note, Jews in Greek-speaking countries, Saloniki and Rhodes, suffered enormously

c0507
00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:05.700
during the Holocaust. A point also...

c0508
00:47:05.700 --> 00:47:13.620
The tradition in France is true that the Sifre Torot, the scrolls of the Tora, came from Spain.

c0509
00:47:14.180 --> 00:47:17.340
And they wanted to protect them because the Germans were just simply destroying them.

c0510
00:47:18.100 --> 00:47:19.980
You know to whom they gave it for protections?

c0511
00:47:20.360 --> 00:47:21.720
The church would not accept them.

c0512
00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:23.360
To the local mosque.

c0513
00:47:23.920 --> 00:47:25.320
And to the credit of the mosque.

c0514
00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:28.320
And credit, they deserve the credit.

c0515
00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:31.320
They not only protected the Sifre Torot,

c0516
00:47:31.320 --> 00:47:37.480
but after the Second World War, it was returned to the members of the community in Rhodes.

c0517
00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:43.120
But again, so therefore you have here a question of morality serving as the basis of legislation.

c0518
00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:50.940
There's other kind of elements of morality introduced into rabbinic jurisprudence.

c0519
00:47:51.660 --> 00:47:54.540
There is what they call lifnim mishurat ha-din.

c0520
00:47:55.200 --> 00:47:58.720
Lifnim mishurat ha-din means when the law at hand,

c0521
00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:02.200
the letter of the law at hand

c0522
00:48:02.200 --> 00:48:06.920
let you get away with murder

c0523
00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.580
let the guilty

c0524
00:48:09.580 --> 00:48:11.600
yes, let the guilty

c0525
00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:14.740
go unpunished

c0526
00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:18.440
now, there are three classes

c0527
00:48:18.440 --> 00:48:20.080
of Lifnim Mishurata Din

c0528
00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:23.460
and there again the judge may use

c0529
00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:25.240
a moral basis

c0530
00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:27.800
for his legal decision

c0531
00:48:27.800 --> 00:48:30.420
The three cases, the three classes are the following.

c0532
00:48:30.700 --> 00:48:37.820
These were very, very clearly stipulated by a Rabbi from Turkey, Shelomo Algazi, 17th century.

c0533
00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:38.620
He says like this.

c0534
00:48:38.860 --> 00:48:39.800
The three cases are the following.

c0535
00:48:40.520 --> 00:48:44.380
Number one, there could be a case in which everybody is obligated to do,

c0536
00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:50.940
but because the individual in question has a special status,

c0537
00:48:51.940 --> 00:48:53.600
he finds himself exempted.

c0538
00:48:53.960 --> 00:48:54.520
An example.

c0539
00:48:54.520 --> 00:49:05.360
According to Jewish law, if you find an animal that was lost, you must take this animal and

c0540
00:49:05.360 --> 00:49:10.940
bring it to a civilized place and eventually you must find the owner.

c0541
00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:20.680
Now somebody could say, well my status, I'm a wealthy person, I'm a distinguished individual,

c0542
00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:22.000
I never deal with donkeys.

c0543
00:49:22.880 --> 00:49:24.440
I don't deal with cows.

c0544
00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:25.480
Alright?

c0545
00:49:25.880 --> 00:49:28.040
So therefore, everybody, yes, is obligated.

c0546
00:49:28.660 --> 00:49:31.200
But I cannot take care of a donkey

c0547
00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:34.260
because it's not part of my social status.

c0548
00:49:36.260 --> 00:49:39.140
This is a case of moral judgment.

c0549
00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:41.140
The judge may say,

c0550
00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:42.220
No, sir.

c0551
00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:43.420
Yes?

c0552
00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:47.440
According to moral,

c0553
00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:48.360
yes, you're right,

c0554
00:49:48.360 --> 00:49:55.360
according to the law we cannot obligate you to do a chore that is outside your social

c0555
00:49:55.360 --> 00:50:05.540
world. But if you are not a Meshurah you must do it. That's one case. Another case is in

c0556
00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:15.400
which not only him but also everybody is exempted. How come? For instance, if a person finds

c0557
00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:22.400
an animal. According to Jewish law, you must take care of the animal for 12 months. After

c0558
00:50:23.660 --> 00:50:27.860
12 months, you sell the animal and you keep the money. It's yours. You assume that the

c0559
00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:34.860
owner gave up hope and therefore it's your property. Now, in some cases, the judge say,

c0560
00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:42.140
well, it's 12 months and one day, 12 months and one week, or maybe the other person could

c0561
00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:48.380
not come. And therefore the judge could tell you on the basis of ethics, of morality, you

c0562
00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:57.380
still must return this property to its lawful owner. And then there is finally an extraordinary

c0563
00:50:57.380 --> 00:51:06.820
case brought in Baba Matian, page 83A. The case refers to Rabbah Bar-Hunna, early 4th

c0564
00:51:06.820 --> 00:51:16.360
century Rabbi. He was in the wine business. The wines were in caskets, clay caskets. He

c0565
00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:22.860
hires people to load the caskets. And the Talmud says they were negligent, they were

c0566
00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:29.700
negligent, and the caskets broke. Now this Rabbi, who was a good lawyer probably, he

c0567
00:51:29.700 --> 00:51:37.160
took the garments of these people as a pawn, yes, as a collateral, as a security, and then

c0568
00:51:37.160 --> 00:51:41.480
forced them to come to court. Now, he claimed two things. I don't have to pay them for the

c0569
00:51:41.480 --> 00:51:48.000
wages, because they were negligent. And moreover, they must pay me for my loss of the wine.

c0570
00:51:49.060 --> 00:51:55.880
And he had the collateral, he had the security, he had the clothing. All right? Now, the person

c0571
00:51:55.880 --> 00:52:01.260
who was the judge at the time, was one of the greatest, greatest juridical minds in

c0572
00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:05.700
rabbinic tradition, was a rab. One of the greatest, the founder of one of the oldest

c0573
00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:12.000
schools, lasted for about a thousand years, the school of Sura. Now, he told them, number

c0574
00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:17.280
one, said the people, they claim, we are hungry, we don't have what to eat, that's what the

c0575
00:52:17.280 --> 00:52:23.640
workers, they claim. So he said to them, to him, not only that they don't have to pay

c0576
00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:31.540
you, but you have to pay their wages. And this is a very important thing. Even when

c0577
00:52:31.540 --> 00:52:40.040
you are losing money, the judge sometimes on the basis of ethics, the case at hand is

c0578
00:52:40.040 --> 00:52:45.080
something special because these workers have nothing to eat. Not only that they don't have

c0579
00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:56.220
to pay, but you have to go and pay their wages. There is another aspect of morality which

c0580
00:52:56.220 --> 00:53:04.680
is terribly interesting. According to Jewish law, it is legally immoral to be cruel. And

c0581
00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:12.960
I tell you how. The case is Kofin Al Middat Sedom. It means to forbid a person to behave

c0582
00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:18.320
like a sodomite. A sodomite for a Jew is somebody who has no compassion to another human being.

c0583
00:53:19.120 --> 00:53:27.840
How? You have a property, a real estate property. You want to sell it. Your next-door neighbor wants

c0584
00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:33.520
to buy it from you because you want to extend his own property. Now, by law, you could sell

c0585
00:53:33.520 --> 00:53:39.040
your property to wherever you want. This man is offering you the same price as you want to sell

c0586
00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:43.000
And you say, I don't want to sell him because I don't like my neighbor.

c0587
00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:44.740
All right?

c0588
00:53:45.260 --> 00:53:45.960
It's a lack of cruelty.

c0589
00:53:46.020 --> 00:53:46.740
I just don't like him.

c0590
00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:47.840
I hate him.

c0591
00:53:48.220 --> 00:53:48.580
All right?

c0592
00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:53.020
Now, kofi namidat sedam means like this.

c0593
00:53:53.840 --> 00:54:02.560
Whoever buys this property is considered as the legal agent, as the shalia, of the neighbor.

c0594
00:54:02.560 --> 00:54:09.120
and therefore automatically would be transferred to the person next door.

c0595
00:54:09.880 --> 00:54:10.120
Why?

c0596
00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:11.900
Because simply,

c0597
00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:17.600
The next door is benefiting,

c0598
00:54:18.400 --> 00:54:20.280
because now he's going to have his property extended,

c0599
00:54:20.420 --> 00:54:21.100
expanding his property,

c0600
00:54:21.620 --> 00:54:23.260
and the seller has nothing to lose.

c0601
00:54:23.860 --> 00:54:27.020
On the basis of morality.

c0602
00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:32.340
Now, in the letter in which Dr. Sherwin,

c0603
00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:39.520
was more or less describing the lectures, the perimeter of the lecture, the subject of the lecture.

c0604
00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:50.040
He suggested that I should try to examine if there is any difference between the Sepharadi and the Ashkenaz approach

c0605
00:54:50.040 --> 00:54:52.560
to this matter of morality and ethics.

c0606
00:54:53.740 --> 00:54:56.360
There is, in a very interesting case.

c0607
00:54:57.280 --> 00:55:02.280
The case was in Toledo, and we happen to know the exact day.

c0608
00:55:02.280 --> 00:55:13.220
1321. Now, we know the exact date. In Toledo, the official legal language was not Spanish

c0609
00:55:13.220 --> 00:55:22.200
among the Jews. It was Arabic. Spanish did not yet develop a legal terminology. And they

c0610
00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:29.320
had Takanot, decrease, communal decrease, in which more or less regulates the property

c0611
00:55:29.320 --> 00:55:36.080
of a wife. Not according to Talmudic law, because again, they thought it was too antiquated,

c0612
00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:43.080
but they made a special communal laws regulating the property of a married woman. Now, the

c0613
00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:50.640
decree was written in classical Arabic. And it pertains to the woman according to this

c0614
00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:57.500
thesis, she has a right to half of her property. The question was raised whether she has a

c0615
00:55:57.500 --> 00:56:02.500
right over half of the property while the husband, even while the husband is alive?

c0616
00:56:02.500 --> 00:56:08.180
Or only if the husband dies or she dies, her heirs have access to half of the property?

c0617
00:56:08.180 --> 00:56:10.180
That was the question.

c0618
00:56:10.180 --> 00:56:15.780
Now there was an individual who was the secretary of the court, a member of the court and the

c0619
00:56:15.780 --> 00:56:19.300
secretary of the court, and his name was Israel de Toledo.

c0620
00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:23.540
He was a distinguished member of the court.

c0621
00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:29.880
The head of the court was perhaps one of the greatest legal minds in Jewish history, Rabbeinu

c0622
00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:35.220
Asher Ashkenaz, was the famous Rabbeinu Asher, the Rosh who came from Germany and settled

c0623
00:56:35.220 --> 00:56:37.220
down in Toledo.

c0624
00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:43.060
Now the question was what this word in Arabic actually meant?

c0625
00:56:43.060 --> 00:56:45.240
What was the semantic of that word?

c0626
00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:47.860
X word, makes no difference.

c0627
00:56:47.860 --> 00:56:59.780
The Rabbeinu Asher interpreted that word in light of the rabbinic lexicon, in light of

c0628
00:56:59.780 --> 00:57:06.980
the Talmud, in light of Jewish semantics.

c0629
00:57:06.980 --> 00:57:09.740
Israel the Toledo says no.

c0630
00:57:09.740 --> 00:57:16.500
The word is written, this term is written in Arabic, and therefore it has to be interpreted

c0631
00:57:16.500 --> 00:57:19.100
according to the universal language of the country.

c0632
00:57:21.300 --> 00:57:23.260
Now, a very interesting case.

c0633
00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:26.000
The document is drawn in Arabic,

c0634
00:57:26.780 --> 00:57:28.740
presented to a Jewish court.

c0635
00:57:29.840 --> 00:57:31.620
How would you interpret this term?

c0636
00:57:32.180 --> 00:57:36.120
According to the general social meaning?

c0637
00:57:36.940 --> 00:57:40.520
Or according to the specificity of Jewish court?

c0638
00:57:42.580 --> 00:57:44.640
Israel de Toreo argued the following.

c0639
00:57:44.780 --> 00:57:45.360
It says like this.

c0640
00:57:45.360 --> 00:57:50.380
It says, being Jewish does not mean to be non-Goy.

c0641
00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:53.440
Very important.

c0642
00:57:53.980 --> 00:57:56.840
Still you are part of general humankind,

c0643
00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:01.240
and therefore you have the same values,

c0644
00:58:02.060 --> 00:58:03.020
the same ideas,

c0645
00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:07.120
of every other non-Jew,

c0646
00:58:08.140 --> 00:58:10.180
plus something else which is Jewish.

c0647
00:58:10.180 --> 00:58:12.840
but being Jewish

c0648
00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:16.220
does not mean that you no longer

c0649
00:58:16.220 --> 00:58:17.220
that you have lost

c0650
00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:18.620
yes

c0651
00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:20.560
all the contact

c0652
00:58:20.560 --> 00:58:22.980
with the non-Jewish world

c0653
00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:24.120
with the universal world

c0654
00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:27.180
he gives an example says

c0655
00:58:27.180 --> 00:58:29.480
we have certain

c0656
00:58:29.480 --> 00:58:31.960
ceremony by which

c0657
00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:33.900
two people contract matrimony

c0658
00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:37.980
the Gentiles do not have the same ceremony

c0659
00:58:37.980 --> 00:58:41.280
He says, it doesn't mean that the genders are not married.

c0660
00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:45.980
It means that they are married, but not according to Jewish law.

c0661
00:58:45.980 --> 00:58:52.980
In other words, we must recognize their institutions, but we say, according to Jewish law, there's something different.

c0662
00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:56.980
Rabbeinu Asher, he says, no.

c0663
00:58:56.980 --> 00:59:00.980
Being Jewish means that you have to think thoroughly Jewish.

c0664
00:59:00.980 --> 00:59:10.220
Jewish. And you cannot apply any value outside of Judaism in the court of law. Because this

c0665
00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:16.260
was a fundamental argument. And that was the biggest difference in the approach to both

c0666
00:59:16.260 --> 00:59:23.880
morality and jurisprudence, separating the Sepharadi from the Ashkenaz legal tradition.

c0667
00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:28.720
Let me give you two very interesting examples, again from the court of law. According to

c0668
00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:37.440
Jewish law, if somebody is an avarian, is a sinner, he cannot serve as a witness. Simply,

c0669
00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:43.900
if he broke one law, it means that he has no respect of the law. Now, one of the laws

c0670
00:59:43.900 --> 00:59:54.140
is forbidden to shave with a razor. Now, in Italy, Jews use razors to shave. Now, are

c0671
00:59:54.140 --> 00:59:55.820
Now these individuals,

c0672
00:59:56.300 --> 00:59:58.560
could we be witnesses or not?

c0673
00:59:59.660 --> 01:00:01.520
The case is brought in front of

c0674
01:00:01.520 --> 01:00:02.800
Rabbi Schacklan Pronte.

c0675
01:00:03.420 --> 01:00:04.300
And he says, very simple,

c0676
01:00:04.540 --> 01:00:08.600
for the person shaving,

c0677
01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:12.040
shaving does not constitute

c0678
01:00:12.040 --> 01:00:13.020
a breaking of the law

c0679
01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:14.740
because he never consented.

c0680
01:00:15.840 --> 01:00:18.080
He never recognized this as a prohibition.

c0681
01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:21.340
Consent is of the essence.

c0682
01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:23.480
And since this individual

c0683
01:00:23.480 --> 01:00:29.300
never consented to this. He really is not a sinner. It's a very important thing he says.

c0684
01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:35.960
And because we are in a society in which this is not considered as a sin, therefore this individual

c0685
01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:44.700
cannot possibly be Pasulai Dud. Something similar is used by Yosef Qaro in treating of the Karahites.

c0686
01:00:44.700 --> 01:00:52.160
The Karahites observes the Shabbat differently than the Rabbinic Jews. And therefore some people

c0687
01:00:52.160 --> 01:00:56.660
You say, well, therefore the Karaites are not admissible.

c0688
01:00:56.660 --> 01:00:59.020
A Karait cannot testify in the court of law,

c0689
01:00:59.020 --> 01:01:03.320
because from the point of view of rabbinics,

c0690
01:01:03.320 --> 01:01:05.780
they are desecrating the Shabbat.

c0691
01:01:05.780 --> 01:01:07.920
So Maran Joseph Karaites says no.

c0692
01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:11.760
Since from their point of view, it does not

c0693
01:01:11.760 --> 01:01:20.920
constitute a desecration, it is not considered as ..

c0694
01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:23.180
they are, from that point of view, they are not breaking the law.

c0695
01:01:23.900 --> 01:01:24.140
All right?

c0696
01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:27.200
Now, a final point.

c0697
01:01:28.640 --> 01:01:31.700
And this is another controversy between Sepharadi and Ashkenaz legal procedure.

c0698
01:01:33.420 --> 01:01:40.420
Could the law, could the court force morality in general?

c0699
01:01:40.920 --> 01:01:41.520
Yes or no?

c0700
01:01:42.080 --> 01:01:44.100
Things which are outside the scope of the law.

c0701
01:01:46.020 --> 01:01:49.740
Matters of morality, of pure morality, which are not connected, again, with the letter

c0702
01:01:49.740 --> 01:01:50.840
or the text of the law.

c0703
01:01:50.920 --> 01:01:55.160
And in this we have two different opinions.

c0704
01:01:55.160 --> 01:02:01.040
Maran Yosef Qaro, the highest authority in the Sepharadi tradition, says no.

c0705
01:02:01.040 --> 01:02:05.820
The court should not interfere in things which are purely ethical.

c0706
01:02:05.820 --> 01:02:06.820
Encofin.

c0707
01:02:06.820 --> 01:02:15.980
You cannot intervene in things which are purely ethical with no connections to the law.

c0708
01:02:15.980 --> 01:02:22.460
Whereas the Bach, the Ben-Yohai Sirkin says, no, the court may interfere in matters which

c0709
01:02:22.460 --> 01:02:26.940
are of pure morality with no relations to the law.

c0710
01:02:26.940 --> 01:02:30.540
Let me summarize everything I said.

c0711
01:02:30.540 --> 01:02:34.180
Jewish law and Jewish ethics.

c0712
01:02:34.180 --> 01:02:38.240
In Western tradition, there is an opposition between these two terms.

c0713
01:02:38.240 --> 01:02:43.780
And one must select which one is the highest level for him or her.

c0714
01:02:43.780 --> 01:02:59.460
There is a tension. In Judaism there is nothing which is totally, totally, there is no opposition between the two things because we reject the notion of a hierarchical authority. Everything has to be done with consent. Consent means we have to negotiate.

c0715
01:02:59.460 --> 01:03:01.500
and even in the law

c0716
01:03:01.500 --> 01:03:03.780
even the laws which are purely purely

c0717
01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:04.600
biblical

c0718
01:03:04.600 --> 01:03:06.460
with no ambiguity

c0719
01:03:06.460 --> 01:03:09.900
still if the individual in case

c0720
01:03:09.900 --> 01:03:11.120
because of social reasons

c0721
01:03:11.120 --> 01:03:13.060
like in the case of the Jews in Italy

c0722
01:03:13.060 --> 01:03:15.480
or because of theological reasons

c0723
01:03:15.480 --> 01:03:18.100
like the Karais does not recognize this

c0724
01:03:18.100 --> 01:03:19.780
as an obligation

c0725
01:03:19.780 --> 01:03:22.340
therefore this is not a law for this individual

c0726
01:03:22.340 --> 01:03:24.260
and this individual cannot

c0727
01:03:24.260 --> 01:03:25.180
cannot

c0728
01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:28.340
make accountable for that particular law

c0729
01:03:28.340 --> 01:03:35.840
That's number one. Number two, there is something terribly important for the Hebrew mind.

c0730
01:03:35.840 --> 01:03:40.500
It's a question of justice, and justice transcending the letter of the law.

c0731
01:03:41.220 --> 01:03:50.660
Judges and lawyers know that every case may present a challenge to the strict interpretation of the law.

c0732
01:03:51.520 --> 01:03:57.980
In those cases, Jewish tradition has it that morality is of the essence.

c0733
01:03:58.340 --> 01:04:07.840
It's not important only to be just and legally correct, but the judge must be also ethically right.

c0734
01:04:08.840 --> 01:04:12.160
Ethics is essential to the rendering of judgment.

c0735
01:04:13.420 --> 01:04:18.660
Ethical to the point that sometimes we have to choose between levels of morality.

c0736
01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:23.040
And again, permit me to bring you a case from the Talmud.

c0737
01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:27.840
The Talmud refers to this as the case of the Hasid Shoteh.

c0738
01:04:28.340 --> 01:04:30.400
means a stupid,

c0739
01:04:31.820 --> 01:04:32.580
pious individual.

c0740
01:04:33.860 --> 01:04:34.440
Hasid Shoteh.

c0741
01:04:34.920 --> 01:04:36.560
Hasid Shoteh is

c0742
01:04:36.560 --> 01:04:39.000
an individual who sees a woman

c0743
01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:40.780
drowning in a river.

c0744
01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:42.560
She is naked.

c0745
01:04:44.180 --> 01:04:44.700
His

c0746
01:04:44.700 --> 01:04:45.980
moral code

c0747
01:04:45.980 --> 01:04:48.580
forbids him

c0748
01:04:48.580 --> 01:04:51.200
to come in touch

c0749
01:04:51.200 --> 01:04:52.340
to touch a naked woman.

c0750
01:04:54.180 --> 01:04:54.440
Alright?

c0751
01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:55.740
Therefore,

c0752
01:04:56.140 --> 01:04:57.920
if you're going to be too ethical,

c0753
01:04:58.340 --> 01:05:04.640
You're going to let this poor lady drown to death.

c0754
01:05:04.640 --> 01:05:07.520
The Talmud considers this as Hashish Shoteh.

c0755
01:05:07.520 --> 01:05:17.060
Sometimes you have to suppress your own morality for a more meaningful ethics.

c0756
01:05:17.060 --> 01:05:27.500
Finally, as Jews, are we part of a larger society called humanity?

c0757
01:05:27.500 --> 01:05:34.500
or no? And this is two different traditions. Let me defend the Sepharadi tradition. I think

c0758
01:05:35.080 --> 01:05:39.620
to me, because I happen to live in America, in a part of this world, it makes a lot of

c0759
01:05:39.620 --> 01:05:46.620
sense. We, as Jews, are part of something larger than ourselves. And therefore, in a

c0760
01:05:48.600 --> 01:05:55.600
sense, the judge, the student of the law, is not only duty bound to apply any kind of

c0761
01:05:57.500 --> 01:06:07.000
His or her specific Jewish tradition was also to take cognizance of mankind at large,

c0762
01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:12.580
of our own social and intellectual heritage.

c0763
01:06:13.720 --> 01:06:16.680
Again, I want to thank you very much for the opportunity to speak here.

c0764
01:06:17.920 --> 01:06:19.520
It's a complex subject.

c0765
01:06:20.320 --> 01:06:25.000
I told Dr. Sherwin it's the most difficult subject that I had to discuss,

c0766
01:06:25.600 --> 01:06:27.000
but it has been a pleasure.

c0767
01:06:27.500 --> 01:06:28.560
Thank you very much.

